# A subject of 997 letters and one blank, 998 bytes, the most allowed.  The
# value does not fit after "Subject:", so it folds, and the line after the
# fold is the 997 letters with the blank after them, 999 bytes: one more
# than RFC 5322 allows (section 2.1.1), and with the line break, over the
# 1000 bytes of a line in SMTP (RFC 5321, section 4.5.3.1.6).  The expected
# file records what the library writes today, not what it should write.
mode=accept
tls=none
form=(From=alice@example.com To=bob@example.net Body=$'Hi\n' User=alice Password=secret
      Subject="$(printf 'w%.0s' $(seq 997)) ")
